(LESSON 1  FOUNDATIONS) Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(Start with the problem this discipline solves. In Canva Design for Beginners, define the audience, desired outcome, constraints, and a small first experiment.) Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(Write down what success looks like before opening a tool. Use a folder with source files, exports, notes, and a README.) Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 10 Tf
() Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(LESSON 2  VOCABULARY AND TOOLS) Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(Learn the terms used by practitioners of Canva Design for Beginners. Create a glossary in your notes and install or open one recommended tool. Do not collect tools without practising with them.) Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(Save an original source file and an exported copy. Record the file type, size, dimensions, and the reason you chose each setting.) Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 10 Tf
() Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(LESSON 3  A REPEATABLE PROCESS) Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(Brief ’ research ’ sketch ’ first version ’ feedback ’ revision ’ quality check ’ delivery. This sequence protects you from polishing the wrong idea.) Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(Set a 25-minute timer and produce three rough options before selecting one. Compare them against the brief, not personal preference.) Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 10 Tf
() Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(LESSON 4  STEP-BY-STEP PRACTICE) Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(1. Write the user or client goal. 2. Collect two useful references. 3. Break the outcome into small parts.) Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(4. Build the simplest working version. 5. Test it with a real person. 6. Revise one issue at a time.) Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 10 Tf
(7. Export using the required format. 8. Document the result and what you would improve next.) Tj
0 -18 Td
/F1 10 Tf
() Tj
